"Chinese medicine describes yin and yang as connected through four different kinds of relationship, and each one answers a different question about how change happens in the body. The first is opposition: yin and yang restrain each other, the way cold restrains heat and heat restrains cold. If one grows unchecked, it naturally holds the other back. The second is interdependence: neither one can exist without the other, the way a shadow cannot exist without light. Yin provides the substance that yang acts on; yang provides the activity that keeps yin from becoming stagnant.": "4f55a6f20fbd",
"Chinese medicine describes yin and yang as connected through four different kinds of relationship, and each one answers a different question about how change happens in the body. The first is opposition: yin and yang restrain each other, the way cold restrains heat and heat restrains cold. If one grows unchecked, it naturally holds the other back. The second is interdependence: neither one can exist without the other, the way a shadow cannot exist without light. Yin provides the substance that yang acts on; yang provides the activity that keeps yin from becoming stagnant. The third relationship is called waxing and waning: yin and yang do not stay fixed — they shift back and forth constantly, the way day gradually gives way to night and back again. A small increase in one is normally matched by a small decrease in the other, keeping the overall balance steady. The fourth relationship is transformation: under the right conditions, yin can convert into yang and yang can convert into yin, the way water can become steam and steam can become water again. This is the relationship that explains sudden change — a long illness marked by cold, quiet symptoms (yin) can, at a certain point, suddenly flip into a hot, agitated state (yang), and Chinese medicine treats this as a known pattern, not a mystery. These four relationships are not four separate rules to memorize in isolation. They describe one continuously moving system, seen from four different angles: how the two hold each other in check, how they depend on each other, how they shift gradually, and how they can suddenly flip. Together, they explain why \"balance\" in Chinese medicine is never a fixed point — it is a moving target that a healthy body keeps hitting, over and over, without ever settling into one extreme.": "bbfbff637932",
